RISC-V: an inline allocation that does not fit in the heap throws OutOfMemoryException (applies after perf-94).

The inline allocation of an object (perf-26) and of an array (perf-49) ended the guest when the new
bump pointer reached the floor of the heap; the allocation helper throws OutOfMemoryException there,
which a program can catch. The guard is now the condition of a QMARK whose other branch is the
helper call the allocation would have been: the helper allocates from the same heap, below the same
bump pointer, so it throws. The call is marked as not returning, which keeps the register allocator
out of its way: nothing is live after it.

A QMARK is legal only as the whole value a statement stores to a local. An object allocation always
is one, and so is an array allocation when the object allocator phase meets it, which is where arrays
are expanded now; the preamble of the sequence (the pointers, and for an array the length and its
guard) goes into a statement of its own before it. An array allocation that is first met in morph,
inside a tree, keeps the guard that fails fast.

The fast path is the same instructions. The temporaries of the sequence now live across a branch,
which can leave a loop with several allocation sites one register short for a hoisted constant
(on a loop with four sites: 69 steps an iteration where it was 60). JitZkAllocFallback=0 restores
the guard that fails fast.

The Checked JIT also names the overflow and fail-fast bounds checks in its dumps, where it asserted.

Regression test: bflat-ts:memory/array_length_checks
Regression test: bflat-ts:memory/array_length_checks_helper

